<p>Traffic through Atocha builds noticeably around Friday afternoons, Sunday evenings, and the long weekends the Spanish calendar is full of, known locally as puentes, when large numbers of Madrileños travel to and from the capital at once. If your onward flight falls on one of these dates, it's worth allowing a little extra time for both the station itself and the roads leading out to the airport, since congestion around Atocha and the M-30 tends to be heavier than on a typical weekday. A private transfer booked in advance sidesteps the added pressure of finding a taxi during exactly these peak windows, when demand at the rank can noticeably outstrip supply.</p>
